WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:10.120
On behalf of our native country
I have said

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.040
that she is our favourite opponent
in the ski tracks.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.640
You're kind of from the forest yourself.
You're born and raised in Lillehammer.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.840
Yes, the forest and the mountains.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.080
The Birkebeiner race over the mountain.
Yes, I go skiing a lot.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.360
It's what I love to do the most.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.960
-How extreme are you?
-Well, no, I'm...

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.120
No, I'm rather a harmonious skier,
that's what I've become over the years.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.200
I do it to get some peace of mind.
I've wondered what it is.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.320
I think it's that you follow tracks
and you can't go wherever you want to.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.720
But then your mind can wander.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.880
So I have solved
personal problems and book problems.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.880
"What should I name this chapter?"

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.320
I solve my life problems while skiing.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.120
We're going to talk
about something more serious.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.720
Your bestseller "One Of Us" is about
Anders Behring Breivik and his victims.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.520
It has got international attention.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.400
This week Åsne followed him
in court once again.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:33.120
It is a national trauma,
we're mourning...

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.960
-You didn't speak to him for this book.
-He wrote to say he didn't want to.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.640
This factors all together
not one dramatic answer.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.440
His childhood is probably
what he lies about the most.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.200
He has a long story of rejections
in his life.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.080
He was one of us.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.320
Anders Behring Breivik has sued
the Norwegian government

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.360
because of inhuman prison conditions.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.520
You're covering the trial
for international press agencies.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:14.240
How was it for you? You followed
the first trial for ten weeks.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.560
How was it to see him again?
You've come straight from the court.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.520
I was ambivalent about going there.
I hadn't planned to go.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:33.120
But it was happening here again,
and I thought: "I'll follow this case."

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.440
All the journalists were anxious.
We hadn't seen him for four years.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.360
"How has he changed?"
But when we saw him,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.360
he turned out to be exactly the same.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.880
He certainly didn't have
any isolation damages.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:57.680
He was himself, but even more
absorbed in his political project.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.960
What he wants is a stage,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.640
a place to share his thoughts
and spread his message.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:12.680
And to gain more supporters.
That became very clear this week.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.240
So it's mostly attention
he wants to get from this?

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.400
That was very clear.
His case fell apart this week.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.320
It's obvious that the government
will win this round.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.280
When he was asked about
his alleged isolation damages,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.080
he had to search for the words.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:40.920
He mentioned headaches
and being a bit depressed now and then.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:45.400
He couldn't even
come up with something

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.440
which could have been a result
of being in isolation for so long.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.520
He is someone who sought isolation
before the terror attack.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.440
He's not a very social person.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.680
Reports by doctors and psychiatrists
were presented in court.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.240
According to them, he had said in prison
that he had never been happier.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.000
He was very pleased
with his three cells.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.920
So he is quite happy.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.000
This was his only way to be heard.
That's why he sued the government.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.800
When you sit there... There are
journalists there and yourself.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:30.160
What's the atmosphere like?
Do people feel provoked?

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.240
At times. He had three hours
were he could speak freely.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.760
I've got to know him a little bit
while working on the book for two years

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.800
and spending ten weeks in court.
You get used to the way he speaks.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.440
After three hours you've had enough.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.880
And I thought: "Can someone
just turn off the microphone?"

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:59.400
Nobody does that of course.
He's allowed to be heard.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:03.280
But he's very manipulative

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.640
towards his own team and the judge.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:14.720
He wants to talk about things
that don't relate to the case.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.880
He went straight on to politics.
The judge let him speak.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.760
I guess it was to get
a clearer picture of

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.960
how he is
when he is allowed to speak freely

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:35.480
to get an understanding of
whether he has been damaged.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:42.400
You called him manipulative,
but who is being manipulated?

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:48.040
He manipulates situations a lot.
He gets his way in the end.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:53.240
Are we manipulated as well?
The media? We're talking about him.

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.840
To a certain degree, but I think
the media has been responsible

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.840
both in the first trial
and this second one.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Little has been written
about him in between.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:09.640
There's been a lot the last few days.
Next week there will be less.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:15.520
We can't think: "It's unwise
to report the worst things he says."

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.280
Those things expose him.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.680
He can't differentiate between
what's important and not.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.520
He can't put it into context
with the crimes he has committed.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.520
He talks about plastic cutlery
as a torture like existence,

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.680
or about cold coffee and waiting
20 minutes before being let out.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.200
Like it's bad room service at a hotel.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:49.080
He's not able to judge what...

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:55.400
He's not able to read social cues.
That's where he fails in a court.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.080
But he will always try
to use the situation

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:04.800
to get the best place
or to get you to listen one more hour.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:11.360
That's also something I noticed
in the letters I received.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.400
-You got letters from him?
-Yes. I asked for an interview thinking:

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.360
"Is it right to interview him?"
Yes, it's a book about him.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.840
He wrote back: "Dear Åsne..."
and was very flattering.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:33.400
"But I see you as a predator.
You will stab me harder than others."

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.920
"But I can work with you..."
There were lots of "buts".

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:44.480
"...if I get to write half the book."
He would do the middle and I the rest.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:48.760
-Then he would give an interview.
-He wanted to co-write it.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.760
Has he read your book?

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:59.920
He sent me a handwritten letter:
"Could you please send me a copy?"

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.240
It was strange, because when
I was writing it I was thinking:

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:08.880
"He is bloody well going to read this."
About the people he killed.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:13.040
About the innocent youths
he didn't even know who were.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.480
However, when the book was finished,
I really didn't want him to read it.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.200
So I never sent him a copy,
but I assume his solicitor got him one.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:29.960
Some researchers were meeting him
and his condition was

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:35.360
that Åsne Seierstad's name
and the book were never mentioned.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.080
Then I knew he had read it
and had not liked what he read.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:50.120
His mother, who is no longer alive,
you met her for your book.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.480
You were one of few who met her,
right before she died.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.480
She was mentioned again
in this trial as well,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.720
things that he had said
about his mother.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.320
Did they have
the same understanding of reality?

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:14.400
They have a very strange relationship,
which they have had all their lives.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:21.560
She is the one who was responsible
for him being neglected as a child

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:26.000
which caused him damage early on.
This has been documented.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:32.640
Love and hate. "I wish you were dead."
Then she's cuddling him in bed.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.520
Later in life the power balance turns.
Now he has the power.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:43.000
He tells her not to walk next to him,
fearing he'll be seen as mentally ill.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:49.160
"Don't stand so close to me, mum."
She told me this is what he said.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:54.760
When I met her
she only had one week left of her life.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:59.720
She knew that. I believe she wished
to make sort of a testament.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.880
She said she felt that Norwegians
should forgive him, like she had done.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:10.440
I thought:
"She's got no right to say that."

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.360
He's never asked for forgiveness
or shown remorse.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:20.400
She also told me about his childhood,

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:28.040
the reality she wanted to portray,
things that also had been good.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.560
In the trial he said he hoped
she wouldn't be called as a witness.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.080
He said his mother is his Achilles heel.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.680
There is something that she knows.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:45.680
He also refused to see her
right before she died

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.240
because she wasn't proud enough
of what he had done.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:55.320
I don't think we will ever
get to the bottom of that relationship.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.640
He wrote nasty things
about his mother in the "manifesto".

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:06.000
I believe this love/hate relationship
lasted their whole lives.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.680
At the same time she's the one
who knows him the best.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.720
Åsne, thank you very much for coming.

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:17.760
I'd like you to stay.
We have more guests. Thank you.

